A||||11||Darren's 87 coupe||88 duke in 87 coupe....Ogre or anyone?||||5||
Z||000000||Darren's 87 coupe||10-15-2001||09:02 PM||||i am looking at getting an 88 Iron Duke from Fieromaster88. I have a 5 speed 87 coupe and his is an from an automatic 88 coupe.  will this be a plug and play swap or will i have to swap some things out?  I think the 87 and 88 are the same but not sure.  I know I should go for a 6 but lets leave that alone.  i need the better gas mileage if you can believe that. Man I must be getting sick to think that way.  Someday I'll get the v8 dropped in it.<BR>Darren||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||10-15-2001||09:05 PM||||should be plug and play.<P>If it hasn't already been done, consider replacing the belt tensioner while it's out.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||10-15-2001||09:12 PM||||Everything I've ever heard leads me to believe that the 87 and 88 Duke are identical with the exception of the 88's internal balancer. I've even heard that some of the late 87's *had* the balancers. Shouldn't be a problem. Not sure about the flywheel, but I *think* they should be interchangeable, too. I second the belt tensioner, too. It's a real chore to get to, in the car. <BR>FWIW... I have an 88 Duke with an automatic. It runs great for 98 HP, but it's gas mileage isn't *that* much better than my automatic Formula. If the Formula had the economy gearing that the coupe has, I suspect the mileage would actually be better than the Duke. <BR>As always, YMMV.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||10-15-2001||09:34 PM||||Didn't the 87 dukes have the external oil filter? I thought it was only the 88 that had that strange one that was in the oil pan.||||reg||5
Z||000004||Raydar||10-16-2001||12:55 PM||||Ooops. Yup. The 87's had an external oil filter.<BR>When GM went to the internal balancers, they put the filter in the pan at the same time.<BR>The engines still should be interchangeable.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-17-2001||07:43 PM||||The oil filter on the 87 is external but that's not an issue really. The pan filter in the 88 may acutally be easier to change as long as the plug is in good shape. Even if it's in crappy shape Lisle has a tool for it. <P>I hate changing the external filter on mine. It always dumps oil down everything. Allot of oil does stay in the filter but there's all the oil between the filter face and the recess in the block... It's enough to make a pretty good mess. There's just not allot of room to get a rag in there to catch it. Most of it end up running down the engine mount bracket and onto the mount. Not good for the mounts to be soaked in oil.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Master_Sushi||10-18-2001||10:18 AM||||I liked the internal oil filter in my 88 coupe. Made changing oil really fast. I think I had it down to less then 8 mins or so. It was kind of messy though, sometimes the oil filter would fall out(or I'd drop it) and it would make quite a mess. It's a very small filter, its probably best to change them with every oil change as I did. <P>The filter on my 84SE is HUGE, Im thinking I might have got the wrong one because to the top of full on the dipstick was about 5qrts of oil or so!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||10-18-2001||10:49 AM||||I'm not too knowing about the 4's but isn't the fan computer controlled on the 88's whereas eariler years have a switch in the block??||||reg||1
Z||000008||BN Boomer||10-18-2001||12:32 PM||||I swapped a 2.5 from an 88 with the 5 speed into my 87 with an automatic. It was pretty much plug and play. As has been noted, the 88 has the internal balance shafts and the internal oil filter. They are both balanced the same and use the same flywheels/flexplates. The motor mounts are different, but this shouldn't be an issue as you will be re-using your old ones anyhow. I did also find that they had different oil pressure sending units, however. The harness in the 87 would not plug into the sending unit of the 88 engine so I simply had to swap sending units with the old 87 engine. Worked fine after that.||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroJoe||10-18-2001||12:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>I'm not too knowing about the 4's but isn't the fan computer controlled on the 88's whereas eariler years have a switch in the block??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, with the DIS engines, both 87 and 88, the computer controls the coolant fan.<P>Also, another note with DIS engines, is that they both used a new style, variable displacement AC compressor.  Once you turn the AC on in the car, the compressor runs full time, varying the pressure with valves inside of itself.  That way, you don't have the loss of power surge when the compressor clutch engages as you did with earlier cars.  You just have the lack of power full time.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Darren's 87 coupe||10-18-2001||01:43 PM||||hey boomer and joe thanks for that info, those are 2 things i hadn't heard yet and will help me out.  Except the compressor sounds like something i just have to live with, but roll down them windows and full power is restored!  all this is very very helpful guys and i'll be needing more advice soon so keep it coming if you know more.  <BR>Darren||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||10-18-2001||07:23 PM||||I forgot about the oil sender...<P>Just buy a new 87 oil sender and be done with it... It's allot easier to change them with the engine out. It's cheap insurance against the old one failing and blowing oil all over.<P>The power loss on the V5 ac compressor isn't bad. It's way better than the old A6 family. The V5 rarely runs at full displacement in a system that's working right.||||reg||1
